Flecha: Spaces with separate cookies and logins.

The Electron spike gives such a Space its own persistent session partition
(session.fromPartition("persist:space-<id>"), src/main/sessions.ts). The
Chromium equivalent here is a persistent StoragePartition per Space, fixed
on each of the Space's tabs through its SiteInstance
(SiteInstance::CreateForFixedStoragePartition), which the shell asks for with
chrome.flechaPrivate.createTab({partition}).

Why a storage partition and not a Profile per Space: a Profile has its own
extension system, so the shell (and its one Store of Spaces), the window
frame, Flecha's shortcuts and every other Space would have to be split or
bridged across profiles, and a Chromium window belongs to one profile, so a
Space's tabs couldn't share the window with the others. A StoragePartition
keeps everything else (the window, the shell, extensions, history, the
password manager, prefs) and gives the tab its own cookies, localStorage,
IndexedDB, service workers and cache, on disk under
<profile>/Storage/ext/flecha-space/, as <webview> partitions are. Chromium
already keeps a fixed partition across navigations, cross-site ones and new
BrowsingInstances included (RenderFrameHostManager, NavigationRequest), and
Chrome on Android opens real tabs this way, so pages behave as in any tab.

What this patch adds:
- chrome/browser/flecha/flecha_spaces.h: the partition of a Space, by name.
- flechaPrivate.createTab / getTabPartition / clearPartition.
- Tabs opened from a page stay in its partition: noopener windows
  (WebContentsImpl::CreateNewWindow, which made them in a default
  SiteInstance), links to a new tab and "Open Link in New Tab"
  (CreateTargetContents, asked of the initiating frame), and Peek.
- The partitions survive Chromium's storage partition garbage collection.

